This study explores the integration of mobile devices with Learning Management Systems in higher education, focusing on student perspectives. As technology continues to reshape educational practices, the ability to learn anytime and anywhere has become increasingly feasible. The core objective of this research is to investigate how combining mobile technologies with LMS can create a new trend in the learning environment—one that enhances accessibility, engagement, and flexibility in education. With 93 students from the Department of Computer Information Systems at Near East University participating, the study aims to understand students’ perceptions of this integrated approach, and whether factors like gender or academic performance influence those views.

The research addresses a critical gap in educational technology: while LMS platforms are widely used, they often lack dynamic instructional features and are typically accessed via desktop or laptop computers, limiting their reach. At the same time, mobile learning—or m-learning—offers opportunities for continuous, on-the-go education through devices like smartphones and tablets. The central hypothesis is that integrating LMS with mobile technology will significantly enhance the learning experience by making it more accessible, collaborative, and motivating for students.

To evaluate this, the study employed a structured questionnaire titled “Students’ perceptions of the new trend learning environment,” which was validated by experts and demonstrated high reliability with a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.96. The survey collected demographic data and measured student perceptions across 14 items using a five-point Likert scale. Participants included both male and female students, as well as those categorized as high-level and low-level performers based on GPA. Data were analyzed using SPSS 16.0, applying descriptive statistics, independent samples t-tests, and measures of central tendency to assess responses and identify potential differences among groups.

The findings reveal overwhelmingly positive student perceptions of the mobile-integrated LMS environment. Key results show high mean scores across all 14 items, with particular emphasis on enjoyment, accessibility, and collaboration. For instance, students strongly agreed that the system was enjoyable and that they would like to see it used in all their courses—both scoring a mean of 4.93. The ability to access course content anytime and anywhere received a mean of 4.88, underscoring the value of flexibility. Similarly, motivation from using a novel teaching tool and the availability of prompt feedback through online assessments were also highly rated. Notably, collaborative learning opportunities scored very high, reinforcing the importance of peer and instructor interaction in digital environments.

Importantly, the study found no statistically significant differences in perceptions based on gender or academic performance level. Both male and female students expressed nearly identical views, with mean perception scores of 4.44 and 4.46 respectively—no significant difference at the p > 0.05 level. Likewise, high-achieving and lower-performing students showed similar levels of acceptance and satisfaction, suggesting that the benefits of mobile LMS integration are broadly applicable across diverse student populations.

In conclusion, the research strongly supports the idea that integrating mobile devices with LMS creates a powerful, future-oriented learning platform. Students not only embrace this model but actively desire its expansion across all courses. The study highlights the transformative potential of mobile learning in fostering flexible, engaging, and collaborative education. It suggests that institutions should prioritize the development of mobile-compatible LMS solutions to meet evolving student needs. Future work could explore long-term learning outcomes, usability across different disciplines, and the role of specific mobile features like notifications or offline access.

Among its strengths, the study uses a reliable instrument and clearly demonstrates high user satisfaction. However, limitations include a relatively small, single-institution sample and self-reported data, which may affect generalizability. Despite these constraints, the research makes a compelling contribution to the field of educational technology by validating student enthusiasm for mobile-integrated learning and advocating for its broader adoption in higher education.